# Trivy vulnerability exceptions — one CVE ID per line, with a comment
# explaining why it's safe to ignore. Prefer fixing over adding entries
# here; only add when there's a genuine, documented reason a real finding
# doesn't apply.

# --- apps/api image: transitive Debian dependencies pulled in by
# installing Google Chrome itself (`playwright install chrome` — see
# apps/api/Dockerfile). Google Chrome's own .deb package installs with its
# full Recommends: chain (fonts, perl, glib, cups, ncurses, util-linux...)
# the same way `playwright install --with-deps` did — switching from the
# generic Chromium build to the specific "chrome" channel the FFF scraper
# requires (fff-scraper.service.ts uses `channel: 'chrome'`) didn't reduce
# this, it's inherent to installing Chrome's actual package on Debian.
# `apt-get upgrade` (already run in the Dockerfile) has nothing newer for
# any of these — Trivy marks them "affected"/"will_not_fix", no Debian
# bookworm fix published. None of it is reachable application attack
# surface: the scraper only ever visits one fixed, trusted FFF domain over
# HTTPS, in pure headless mode (no X server, no user-supplied files,
# no LDAP/SSH/TIFF processing) — this whole dependency chain sits unused.
