# brace-expansion DoS in OUR app's dependency tree (CVE-2026-14257 and its
# mitigation-bypass follow-up) is fixed, not ignored — see the root
# package.json `overrides` entry pinning the minimatch@3.1.5 instance to
# brace-expansion@1.1.18 (same 1.x API, no rewrite needed).
#
# --- apps/api image only: SEPARATE, unrelated vulnerabilities vendored
# INSIDE the npm CLI itself (apps/api/Dockerfile runs `npm install -g
# npm@latest` to patch npm's own bundled tar/picomatch/sigstore versions —
# see that Dockerfile's comment). As of npm@12.0.2 (current latest,
# checked 2026-08-05), npm still vendors brace-expansion@5.0.7 internally
# (CVE-2026-14257 / its later-assigned CVE-2026-69152, fixed only at
# 5.0.9+) and ip-address@10.2.0 (CVE-2026-69192, fixed at 10.3.1+) — and
# the base image's own default npm@10.9.8 is equally affected on
# brace-expansion (vendors 2.0.2, fixed only at 2.1.4+). No published npm
# release currently ships a fix for either; nothing in our control (not
# our package.json, not `overrides` — these live inside the
# globally-installed npm binary, not our dependency tree). Not reachable
# either way: this npm install is only ever used to bootstrap the image
# at build time and for the one `npx playwright install chrome` call
# below it — never invoked at runtime, never fed attacker- or
# HTTP-controlled input. Revisit by checking
# `npm view npm@latest dependencies` — or just rebuilding the image —
# once npm ships a release with both fixed.
CVE-2026-14257
CVE-2026-69152
CVE-2026-69192
